er-field/id #uuid "62f664e1-3397-5fc8-8997-d85966d501de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294281} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e8a5a4c-630b-516e-b8e0-968782ed3ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e8a5a4c-630b-516e-b8e0-968782ed3ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293182} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ff41a97-f9b0-5355-aeb2-b3b9a775f59b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294507}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a88a0577-f66a-514f-9dd3-d71688a75644" :question/math-academy-id "q-276798" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $3,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${240}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=3,\\,\\theta ={240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({240}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040aa995-2a95-5978-ae2e-5bf3e64df54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351604} {:answer/id #uuid "327eef0b-6d33-56b7-b7fa-65647e6064b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352034} {:answer/id #uuid "8813249a-4b08-5911-ab6e-9dfd026fab0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352759} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f4b322-8686-56b2-9216-36cb838b3f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf46c88-e2c1-5c74-915f-85de76f4b18a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f4b322-8686-56b2-9216-36cb838b3f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353432} :answer-field/id #uuid "496d734d-db4b-581d-ac63-001e27aa3e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ab581aa-df37-556d-9193-036f42c477b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8516" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${150}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta ={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},4).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092061 :question/id #uuid "63b5b1e6-1885-554c-973c-bd78555f44ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5385" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(r, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6} \\right)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $\\left(5r,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3,-\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tr &= \\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\[3pt]\n\t\t &=\\sqrt{3^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2}\\[3pt]\n\t\t &= \\sqrt{12} \\[3pt]\n\t\t &= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\t}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(5(2\\sqrt{3}),\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\left(10\\sqrt{3},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx &= r\\cos\\theta\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=10\\sqrt{3} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 10\\sqrt{3} \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= -15\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ty &= r\\sin\\theta\\[5pt]\n\t\t&=10\\sqrt{3} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 10\\sqrt{3} \\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t&=5\\sqrt{3}\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $\\left( -15,  5\\sqrt{3}\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf20096-bc79-5589-a630-1641f8fe0ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{15}, 2\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090419} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c9ff15-216c-5b7c-bf56-2cfa31cb97af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{15}, 2\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090420} {:answer/id #uuid "5d034e1a-b724-530e-affc-63bac67f2a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{5}, 2\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090421} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca499d2-67e9-5fc6-a4c5-5208506dc65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{5}, \\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090422} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0b692a-5390-54a9-a6f7-739b88f06ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{15}, 4\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090423}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4d92fd-ac4b-5f7a-9c34-e0bfcd92fac9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090418}] :question/id #uuid "0ebc9d27-9c03-5dba-a9e8-e61d4df48f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-40803" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r, \\frac{π}{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r, \\frac{π}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{10}, \\sqrt{10})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0d74418-5a15-53d0-9b0f-29a8ffb06a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090425} {:answer/id #uuid "decad71a-b4e9-5d7b-9199-8f0aee31dcd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090426} {:answer/id #uuid "e90c9538-684f-5cce-a8ea-7f6831fa103a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090427} {:answer/id #uuid "512c0a28-3bdb-5347-b37e-b43ef2062001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090428} {:answer/id #uuid "55cd377c-4643-5da3-a323-25622bb33cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090429}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7a661a-09c6-5373-b0d4-2134575fd07d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090424}] :question/id #uuid "3dd4897a-0f39-5ff6-8f3b-6202a2c681b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40806" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2, θ)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(1, θ - \\frac{π}{4})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} {:answer/id #uuid "1129da34-d7d1-5e9f-8843-f5403e6bf93a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253820} {:answer/id #uuid "12c049a4-b3f5-57ad-8839-06ff45915613" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253833} {:answer/id #uuid "4009cc1a-951f-501b-bdc2-6e69fb5a6357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254222} {:answer/id #uuid "a9834412-7636-54d2-8673-12769dbe9d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d936aa-147e-55d6-93f2-fbed67c48196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256328}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0e5e2dd-de81-57dc-8848-06ae6eac4cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-120455" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{12})^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=(12,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a02ee7-0c84-56f1-bbf0-ebb90394da60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253859} {:answer/id #uuid "39bbabdf-ded0-54de-9bce-39f8a04d806d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254176} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9d34e5-3a45-5295-96d7-05130d5b7bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254659} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7d44b2-7e30-57fa-9135-30c7aad08a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254758} {:answer/id #uuid "c869f985-2a3d-5a8b-ba06-6ea45dcdacab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39bbabdf-ded0-54de-9bce-39f8a04d806d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254176} :answer-field/id #uuid "8600f656-d47f-52c4-96f7-5d633c8eeb59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d3f790c-6d1a-5380-94bb-56056dda53c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-120468" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2708c61-f2d1-53ee-a4ea-fb782ceca8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2708c61-f2d1-53ee-a4ea-fb782ceca8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293822} :answer-field/id #uuid "da981865-cfbd-52c1-9bab-b92faa7bdbfb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b9cc0e-222c-5712-817e-705a58a6e766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89b9cc0e-222c-5712-817e-705a58a6e766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293467} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef892571-43d2-5d92-9083-6ce12a147796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60b828e5-8a1d-5f25-a3d7-f526a8ab5604" :question/math-academy-id "q-276803" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ddb059-0d05-51a1-bd95-f4bc61d11fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ddb059-0d05-51a1-bd95-f4bc61d11fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293267} :answer-field/id #uuid "34283d07-159f-5091-9a01-99f05e0ae58a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4492581-9f4a-54f6-8d56-efd1c2556ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4492581-9f4a-54f6-8d56-efd1c2556ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293754} :answer-field/id #uuid "a643542b-f211-57cf-9379-abb105ab8ff5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294525}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18267ca6-6217-5e13-9301-7563b567900e" :question/math-academy-id "q-276807" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot 4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=(8,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-4\\sqrt{3},-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c17d61-8b20-57f7-bc5b-640a6ec8d53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c17d61-8b20-57f7-bc5b-640a6ec8d53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293866} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a127bc7-9c4c-53d5-928b-fac60ba066e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6d10ef-69b9-50c0-90b4-1995316eadf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e6d10ef-69b9-50c0-90b4-1995316eadf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292997} :answer-field/id #uuid "826392ad-052e-559d-8fa4-5b8ae6ee49d9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294392}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de1e1e5-b9e0-5943-ba28-afbdac6dbd1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-276838" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(5,0).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=0.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(5,0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8dfee2-0dcc-51dd-8359-792e8b7dcf0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc33d3f-43b1-5036-bee7-64c9f48bbe57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324353} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8acf08-5493-5784-b668-42814866416a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324861} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2836f8-1ba6-5faa-ae75-030fa6b7030c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{5},-3\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325163} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2f2323-45b4-58c8-8c06-7d06b792ddfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5\\sqrt{3},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d8dfee2-0dcc-51dd-8359-792e8b7dcf0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324094} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ba43e9-48cb-5399-8b07-ea56e49d1700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326601}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "351973f9-e651-502b-90b1-ba68a5d4bedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-40804" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{10})^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de63e38-80fb-54c4-9a7c-5a96600b508f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324222} {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} {:answer/id #uuid "2e4dd8b9-9d94-588f-a6d0-7d91017750d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324340} {:answer/id #uuid "81916b05-53d5-5cfe-b7c6-b0642b2c06ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325056} {:answer/id #uuid "82355246-8fcd-5520-a5e5-83b3cb9b05c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d41aa2-c3a0-5270-abba-3a1f3207c3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324266} :answer-field/id #uuid "3036c726-f5d4-51ab-a7e4-77d619c6072b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "417c4b2b-c91e-563b-b92c-41495ea7b2b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-40826" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d257c92-35f5-5542-8460-7eedd2b1d612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352271} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbf2f2-dbb2-5231-a8e3-5c7f27e6aec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352400} {:answer/id #uuid "66bc6397-ad23-5f9d-bbbd-4c19753bdac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352476} {:answer/id #uuid "7d900d5b-7506-5283-a33a-77e549a766d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352669} {:answer/id #uuid "af70cc0a-2339-58cb-ad6d-c2731efb201f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbf2f2-dbb2-5231-a8e3-5c7f27e6aec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352400} :answer-field/id #uuid "d717a7d4-1e96-542f-89a3-19928481d9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354182}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9b63e65-54e5-5242-a627-cab8a091bffb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86619" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$  Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}]}
